{:edb/db-before-t 948 :edb/db-after-t 949 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534261 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:22:01.504Z" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184095 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\n- Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- The matrix ![](images/a3/a33b8a531dc91b49f01826cd7fcedaae2ee3144b5205bbe724efa9f29a302b37.png) has one negative singular value." 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186184095 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\n- Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- The matrix ![](images/b8/b8601b1e66ef1d302e7194f262f6a513063bba2114fb063c2fc16542d4684392.svg) has one negative singular value." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184096 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/85/85f9ccea6cd39f07174651d07316218edf50041f9d373e22cdaedf0c779caaf3.svg)" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184096 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/9a/9a78a1681842653fabade3354cc5e885f0bfc32eefa3b56e9d1faf716dc5d97b.png)" 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186184097 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/59/590d5dc32950efa0fce63a5d64a5595210a3ccb08c613e364586dba4822d7314.svg)" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184097 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/5d/5d2eb1db497eeb2f3e24feaa1ac70547a48cc55d1eeea22da91231b1d1275df8.png)" 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416481 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416486 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416482 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416483 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416484 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184404 1050 660.0 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183889 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186184880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416475 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has two non-negative singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Any $6 \\cdot 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n2. Any $10 \\cdot 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n3. The matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186184880 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A= [\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 false] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416439 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416439 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416439 1083 #uuid "058c04c6-70ed-5f40-82ed-2d503073a803" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416440 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416440 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416440 1083 #uuid "21ea85fd-6292-5db7-9dc1-8fed164bc983" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416441 1080 "$8$ and $4$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416441 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416441 1083 #uuid "2c448a4f-2853-5a0a-adb6-34c3fe973ba2" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416442 1080 "$3$ and $2$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416442 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416442 1083 #uuid "392bfe36-8b63-5aac-ad95-fe5e63ae7b56" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416443 1080 "$3,$ $3$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416443 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416443 1083 #uuid "3c2115b7-3deb-582b-a761-e79b45a72ba3" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416444 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416444 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416444 1083 #uuid "4727adce-5afb-5a6e-bae