e] [17592186416480 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416465 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416467 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416473 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1077 "selection" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416448 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416480 1079 #uuid "f8cda2f1-5552-5357-82cc-f1ab61cac36e" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416476 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1069 "q-95266" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1072 #uuid "5650e0d2-585c-5516-892d-5eacef061cb2" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416481 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416474 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1069 "q-95367" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1072 #uuid "97ca7c6b-3d02-595e-9b24-0e63b350342b" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416482 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416477 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1069 "q-139508" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1072 #uuid "ab4ca253-4533-55ea-9cf5-518c8864d1f6" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416483 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416478 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1069 "q-95359" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1072 #uuid "c791e385-b793-500d-9a77-3e468ca25a09" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416484 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416480 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1069 "q-95261" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ are non-negative.\n- Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1072 #uuid "e69dd032-aa5c-5891-a7fc-97b84703e88a" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416485 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416479 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1069 "q-95281" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1072 #uuid "fef86daf-091a-52fa-bcf0-1d2b609bb348" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true] [17592186416486 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534261 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-058c04c6-70ed-5f40-82ed-2d503073a803" 17592186416439 "answer-21ea85fd-6292-5db7-9dc1-8fed164bc983" 17592186416440 "answer-2c448a4f-2853-5a0a-adb6-34c3fe973ba2" 17592186416441 "answer-392bfe36-8b63-5aac-ad95-fe5e63ae7b56" 17592186416442 "answer-3c2115b7-3deb-582b-a761-e79b45a72ba3" 17592186416443 "answer-4727adce-5afb-5a6e-baec-86299311d447" 17592186416444 "answer-4889279a-9659-5dae-8259-b1442e0b8893" 17592186416445 "answer-4ab592cf-fe9a-5d31-b42c-37ad02aab55a" 17592186416446 "answer-4c278b14-28c2-57de-b60a-2a720876d406" 17592186416447 "answer-4d306b2f-e020-5c0b-8b36-318a141afa1a" 17592186416448 "answer-4f699d44-55da-5c4f-a3d1-373644f33ba8" 17592186416449 "answer-514af78b-310e-50a2-b1ef-8f945c043cb2" 17592186416450 "answer-5b060c6a-de3b-54b0-bfda-579bbdcbb6d0" 17592186416451 "answer-5ec6c063-a755-58f5-8636-29649c9e3395" 17592186416452 "answer-72a19815-f7b7-56e9-badf-ea7b9e73091b" 17592186416453 "answer-7ad14134-11d1-5b66-b73a-b208f1547b8a" 17592186416454 "answer-7c2cf85b-73a0-5874-97a9-cfa33e64d6b5" 17592186416455 "answer-862c188a-6765-5375-b045-c13862731467" 17592186416456 "answer-872eaed5-b7dd-5088-8dd0-359491c4f657" 17592186416457 "answer-884aa6f5-9cee-5d6c-a6d2-1e3e9fda993d" 17592186416458 "answer-93dcd708-1476-51b0-ac1f-9db7ca101165" 17592186416459 "answer-949e815d-00ae-5171-94b0-1798b1a30df1" 17592186416460 "answer-9d9dee5f-cd09-5188-9a3c-cde7f4e68a37" 17592186416461 "answer-a08ff49c-3e48-5969-9cc0-4603170a0646" 17592186416462 "answer-b85ee86c-0aac-52bc-b5db-5824de7a6bdc" 17592186416463 "answer-b8b05a8d-1f8d-58